<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palenque</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 226 BCE–c. 799</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Lakamha, B'aakal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Palenque, anciently known as Lakamha, is the site of a Maya <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usumacinta-river">Usumacinta River</a> in the Mexican state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiapas">Chiapas</a>; its ruins date from about 226 BCE to about 799 CE. A medium-sized site, smaller than Tikal or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copan">Copán</a>, it holds some of the finest architecture, sculpture and bas-relief carving the Maya produced. Sacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calakmul">Calakmul</a> in 599 and 611, the city was rebuilt under K'inich Janaab' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinich-janaab-pakal">Pakal</a>, who ruled from 615 to 683 and whose tomb Alberto Ruz Lhuillier found in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-the-inscriptions">Temple of the Inscriptions</a> in 1952. Elite construction in the ceremonial centre ceased sometime after 800, and the site was later abandoned and overgrown by forest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palenque.jpg" alt="Palenque" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ljuba brank at Slovenian Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:055_Palenque_Pakal_Maji_(29).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
